Over the past few decades, various inorganic semiconductor gas-sensitive materials, such as metal oxides, carbon nanotubes, and graphene, have been widely used as semiconducting active layers of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ gas sensor s.11,12 However, most existing inorganic chemiresistive $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ gas-sensing materials typically require high temperatures and relatively high operating voltages to improve sensitivity13,14 as well as postsynthesis modifications and doping to reduce cross-sensitivity due to interference from other gases,15,16 thus limiting device miniaturization and further integration into mobile devices. In contrast, organic semiconductor materials have attracted considerable research interest due to their advantages of adjustable conjugated molecular structures, cost-effective
