fabrication, and selective detection by covalent and/or noncovalent bonding (coordination, hydrogen, dipole−dipole, van der Waals, etc.) with target gases even at room temperature (RT).17,18 As one of the typical organic semiconductors, metalloporphyrins have drawn great attention for the development of miniaturized and integrated chemiresistive sensors,19,20 benefiting from the versatility of the metal center in the porphyrin core, the substitution of functional groups at the porphyrin periphery, distinct electronic characteristics, and their exceptional thermal and chemical stabilities. However, the uncontrollable aggregation and relatively low film conductivity of the porphyrin-based devices restrain effective interactions with analytes, thus leading to low carrier transfer functions of the chemiresistive sensors.21 This is also the main reason that the performance in terms of sensitivity and limit of detection (LOD) of most organic semiconductor $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensors is far behind those of traditional metal oxide gas sensors. Therefore, a combination of unique properties offered by organic and inorganic sensing components in a single heterogeneous material with more exposed sensing active sites, high conductivity, and a clear structure has become one of the research focuses and still remains a challenge in this field.22−24
In recent years, there has been increasing recognition of the significant role played by metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) in the development of chemiresistive sensors.25,26 These frameworks are constructed from organic linkers and inorganic metal nodes (or clusters). The appeal of MOFs lies in their ability to be finely tuned through the selection of organic linkers and the stability offered by inorganic nodes. This design flexibility allows the development of highly efficient and reliable chemoresistive sensors. The features of well-defined porous structures, high stability, and tailored functionalities for MOFs can promote the enrichment of analytes and rapid mass transfer of the analytes.27 This in turn amplifies the current signals and thereby enhances sensitivity.28,29 Furthermore, MOFs with clear structures, and the synergistic effect of organic and inorganic components,30 can not only promote carrier transport and migration but also provide templates for clarifying the sensing mechanism. However, very little has been explored regarding the reasonable selection of the central metal in MPor components for maximizing the sensing properties of the MPor-based MOFs.31
With this background in mind, by using M-tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)-porphyrins (MTCPP, $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, $\mathbf{Ni},$ and $\mathrm{Cu},$ respectively) as the subunits connected via the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, four kinds of MTCPP-based MOFs denoted as PCN-222-M $\mathbf{\partial}\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{\partial}$ Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu, respectively) were constructed (Scheme 1) and fabricated into self-assembled films by the QuasiLangmuir−Shafer (QLS) method on ITO interdigitated electrodes (IDEs)/glass substrate for NO2 detection32 (Scheme S1). The crystal structures and electronic properties of PCN-222-M were determined by a series of spectroscopic and electrical analyses, including scanning electron microscopy (SEM), X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, $I{-}V$ curves, etc. Then, the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -sensing performance of the four PCN-222-M films was analyzed under different concentrations to screen the sensors suitable for different application scenarios. Among them, the PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni films demonstrated exceptional sensitivity $(2209\%\ \mathrm{ppm^{-1}})$ ) with a superlow limit of detection (LOD) and faster response and recovery, respectively, which can be used for the detection of ultratrace amounts of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and rapid detection of $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}$ . The sensing mechanism of PCN-222-M is proposed based on the energy level structure analysis combined with the density functional theory (DFT) calculations, which revealed the essential roles of the M in MPor subunits and $\mathrm{MPor/Zr}_{6}$ cluster in organic/inorganic hybrid materials in tuning the Fermi level and amplifying the sensing response.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Preparation of PCN-222-M. Based on previous reports, we synthesized PCN-222-M $\mathrm{{(M=\Cu,\Ni,}}$ Fe, Co).33 To synthesize $\mathrm{PCN-}222{\cdot}\mathrm{Cu},$ a solvothermal reaction was employed. In a vial, $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{26~mg)}$ , CuTCPP $\left(12{\mathrm{~mg}}\right)$ , and benzoic acid (225 mg) were dissolved in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMA and sonicated for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . The reaction mixture was then heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under stirring and allowed to react for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the resulting PCN-222-Cu powder was collected, washed with fresh DMA and ethanol through centrifugation, and subsequently dried under vacuum. In addition to the synthesis of PCN-222-Cu with CuTCPP, the other three kinds of MOFs were synthesized with NiTCPP, FeTCPP, and CoTCPP, respectively, by the same method.
Fabrication of Gas Sensors. The fabrication process of PCN222-M films involved a straightforward solution-based quasiLangmuir−Shäfer (QLS) technique (Scheme S1):32 First, the PCN222-M powder was dispersed in anhydrous ethanol $\left(3\mathrm{\mg/mL}\right)$ through ultrasonic treatment. A small amount of the dispersion was then gently spread onto the surface of ultrapure water. As the solvent evaporated, PCN-222-M particles self-assembled, forming nanostructures at the water interface. Once ethanol completely evaporated, a dense thin film remained on the water surface. Subsequently, the film was easily transferred from the water surface onto the substrate of ITO interdigitated electrodes (IDEs) by horizontally lifting it, resulting in QLS films of PCN-222-M. These films served as the semiconducting active layers for $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensing.
Generation of Analytes with Desired Concentrations. The mixture is diluted with dry air using two CS200 mass flow controllers to achieve the desired analyte concentrations: $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{air}$ at $20\ \mathrm{ppm},$ ${\cal S}\mathrm{{O}}_{2}/\mathrm{air}$ at $50\ \mathrm{ppm},$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}/\mathrm{air}$ at $100\ \mathrm{ppm},$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{air}$ at $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Throughout the sensor measurements, dry air is consistently chosen as the carrier gas. The total mass flow rates for the analyte and diluted air are 0.1 and $2~\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , respectively.
Current Monitoring. Before exposing the target gas, the gas sensor is stabilized at a constant airflow for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to establish a steady baseline. All sensing tests are carried out by exposing to a known concentration of a target gas such as $\mathrm{NO}_{2},\mathrm{SO}_{2},\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ for $1\ \mathrm{min};$ , and then the carrier gas (air) returns to baseline. In a standard measurement setup, the gas sensor is positioned inside a sealed Teflon chamber. The gas inlet and outlet are connected to a chamber to allow the analyte to pass. These devices are then connected to a precision source/measurement unit system (Keysight B2912A) via Teflon-mounted breadboards within the chamber. The current flowing through the device is monitored using timing amperometric analysis with a precision source/measurement unit system. A voltage of $10\mathrm{~V~}$ is applied, and the current is recorded at regular intervals of 1 s.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of PCN-222-M. The morphology of the PCN-222-M powder prepared by a solvothermal reaction was characterized using SEM. It is not difficult to find that the four kinds of PCN-222-M possessed a regular crystal structure (Figure 1), implying the successful perpetration of PCN-222- M.34 All of the PCN-222-M showed spindle-shaped short rod structures except for $\mathrm{PCN-}222\mathrm{-Ni,}$ which has a multiprismshaped structure. The particle sizes of both PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni (about 770 and $750~\mathrm{nm}$ in length) are found to be larger than those of PCN-222-Fe and PCN-222-Co (about 500 and ${540}\mathrm{nm}$ in length). The difference in both particle size and morphology of the four kinds of PCN-222-M $\mathbf{\dot{M}=C u,}$ $\mathrm{Ni,}$ Fe, Co) should be obviously attributed to the difference in the central metal M of the M-porphyrin (named as MPor) unit among PCN-222-M.35
As displayed in the XRD patterns of PCN-222-M (Figure 2A), the diffraction peaks at 4.8, 7.0, and $9.7^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (220), (201), and (400) crystal planes, respectively, which are in good agreement with previous studies,34 suggesting good bulk phase purity of PCN-222-M. The porosity of PCN-222-M was calculated through $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption−desorption isotherms in Figure 2B. The type IV isotherm of PCN-222-M typically shows a rapid increase at the point $P/P_{0}\mathrm{=}\ 0.3$ , indicating the coexistence of mesopores and micropores. The specific surface area $(\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ increased in the order of PCN-222-Ni $\left(1115\right)<$ PCN-222-Fe (1393) < PCN-222-Co $\left(1474\right)\mathrm{~<~}\mathrm{PCN-}222\mathrm{-Cu}$ (1509) (Table S1), which are consistent with the reported values in the literature.33 Note that although PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni possess similar particle sizes among the four PCN-222-M, PCN-222-Cu possessed the largest specific surface area, while the smallest one was observed for PCN222-Ni. This seems strange at first glance but is easy to rationalize considering the higher enthalpy for gas adsorption of the central metal Cu compared to that of Ni in PCN-222-M, as revealed in the report published by Coskun et al.36,37 Furthermore, the largest specific surface area of PCN-222-Cu was expected to provide sufficient active sites for gas adsorption, which has a positive effect on gas sensing.
The ultraviolet−visible (UV−vis) absorption spectra of four PCN-222-M and respective MTCPP precursors are displayed in Figure 2C. Both PCN-222-M and MTCPP ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{\mathrm{{;}}}$ , Ni, Fe, and Co) in the DMF solution exhibited typical absorption peaks of porphyrin derivatives with the Soret band and $\textrm{\text Q}$ bands at 417−430 and $466{-}674~\mathrm{nm}$ for MTCPP and at $432-$ 455 and $544-585\ \mathrm{nm}$ for PCN-222-M, respectively (Figure 2C and Table S2). Both the Soret and Q bands of PCN-222-M were all red-shifted and broadened compared to those of MTCPP in solution, suggesting an edge-to-edge intermolecular aggregation between MPor units within PCN-222-M.38 Moreover, UV−vis diffuse reflection spectra (DRS) of PCN222-M in the solid powder showed that both the Soret and $\mathrm{\DeltaQ}$ bands (at 425−441 and $510-539~\mathrm{nm}$ ) were significantly blueshifted compared to those of the respective MTCPP precursors (at 462−474 and $513-542\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) (Figure 2D and Table S3). This demonstrated that the coordination effect of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster reduced the intermolecular interaction of MTCPP. Therefore, the MPor in PCN-222-M showed quasi-molecular properties with limited intermolecular interactions, which was more favorable to expose the active site and promote the adsorption of gas molecules.33,39,40
The FT-IR spectra of PCN-222-M and MTCPP were further obtained to investigate the compositions of the prepared samples.41 As illustrated in Figure 2E,F, in comparison with MTCPP, all of the PCN-222-M exhibited an absence of characteristic peaks around $1690~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ bonds) and $1268~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (C−O bonds), whereas a strong peak appears at $1420~\mathrm{{\cm}^{-1}}$ (COO symmetric stretch bond), reflecting the coordination of the carboxyl group to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster in PCN-222-M and further demonstrating the successful preparation of PCN-222-M.42 In addition, taking PCN-222-Cu as an example (Figure S1), the characteristic peak of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ slightly shifted to $\bar{1}004~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ relative to that of CuTCPP $(1001~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}},$ ).43 This further indicated that CuTCPP in PCN-222-Cu possessed reduced intermolecular interactions and quasi-molecular properties, resulting in the exposure of much more active sites for gas adsorption, consistent with the above results of UV−vis spectra and UV−vis DRS.44
Electrical Behaviors and Gas Sensing. To showcase the potential of PCN-222-M in chemiresistive sensors, we evaluated the electron conductivity of the material. This measurement was conducted at room temperature on PCN222-M films deposited using the QLS method onto glass substrates with interdigitated electrodes made of ITO, as described in Scheme S1. In Figure S2, the $I{-}V$ curves exhibit approximate symmetry, indicating a nonlinear behavior that aligns with the space-charge-limited conduction commonly observed in devices based on phthalocyanine/porphyrin-based semiconductors, as reported in previous studies.45,46 The conductivity of PCN-222-M films was calculated, and the corresponding data are summarized in Table S4. It is not difficult to note that the conductivity $\left(\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\right)$ increased in the order of PCN-222-Fe $(1.92\times10^{-5})$ ), PCN-222-Co $2.28\times$ $10^{-5})$ , PCN-222-Ni $(3.23\times10^{-5})$ , and PCN-222-Cu ( $_{4.61\times}$ $10^{-5},$ ). Obviously, the metal ion located at the center of a planar ligand TCPP determines the energy and degeneracy of the MTCPP molecular orbitals and thus band structure, 4 leading to differences in the conductivity of different PCN222-M. The highest conductivity was achieved for PCN-222- Cu among the four different PCN-222-M, which was conducive to the carrier transport efficiency.
In order to further evaluate the gas sensing characteristics of PCN-222-M, four PCN-222-M films were subjected to low concentrations of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ diluted in dry air, under 1 min dynamic exposure at room temperature for comparative investigation. The applied voltage was optimized using a PCN-222-Cu film sensor as a model. As shown in Figure S3, the percent current change at the same $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ concentration ( $\dot{0.06}\mathrm{ppm}$ in this case) is positively correlated with the voltage, but when the voltage is greater than $10\mathrm{~V~},$ , the sensitivity increase is not obvious. Therefore, a $10\mathrm{~V~}$ applied voltage was chosen for further gassensing measurements. In addition, we also found that the chemiresistive sensor (PCN-222-Cu) proposed in this work could operate at voltages as low as $2\mathrm{V}.$ , which has the potential to develop low-power chemiresistive sensors.48 The responsivity (defined as the percentage change in current toward each concentration) is calculated using the formula $\left[{\left({I_{\mathrm{t}}}-{I_{0}}\right)}/{\ I_{0}}\right]\times$ $100\%$ , where $I_{0}$ represents the initial current and $I_{\mathrm{t}}$ represents the current at the end of an exposure cycle. As shown in Figure 3A−D, the four PCN-222-M films showed negative current responses for different concentrations of the electron-accepting gas ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ indicating the n-type nature of each of the PCN-222- M films. There is a good linear relationship between the percentage of current response of the four PCN-222-M sensors and the target gas concentration, and the absolute values of the slope (defined as sensitivity, $\%\ \mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ ) are 122, 140, 121, and
251 for PCN-222-Fe, PCN-222-Co, PCN-222-Ni, and PCN$222\mathrm{-Cu}$ in the range of $0.02{-}0.15~\mathrm{\ppm}$ (Figure S4), respectively, implying the M-related sensing responsivity for the four metalloporphyrin-based MOFs. Among them, the highest sensitivity (slope) was obtained for PCN-222-Cu $(251\%\ \mathrm{ppm}^{-1})$ , which is mainly attributed to the larger specific surface area that provides much more adsorption sites and higher conductivity as well as the stronger interaction between the $\mathrm{{CuPor/Zr}}$ cluster and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ (vide infra) of PCN-222-Cu compared to the other three metalloporphyrin-based MOFs (PCN-222-Fe, -Co, and -Ni). Furthermore, these excellent characteristics make PCN-222-Cu extremely sensitive to ultralow concentrations of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ in the range of $4{-}80~\mathrm{\ppb}$ (Figure 3E,F), with sensitivities up to 2209 and $250\%\ \mathrm{ppm^{-1}}$ to $_{4-20}$ and 30−80 ppb $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ (adjusted $R^{2}$ value ${>}0.98{\ i}$ ), respectively. Significantly, PCN-222-Cu showed an ultrahigh sensitivity of more than $2200\%\ \mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ to ultralow concentrations $\left(4{-}20~\mathrm{ppb}\right)$ of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ upon 1 min exposure and a calculated low detection limit (LOD) of $0.93\mathrm{\ppb}$ , which is the best performance reported so far for $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensors at room temperature by using air as a diluent gas (Table S5). Given the challenges associated with detecting lower concentrations of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ which can lead to long-term health issues, this device shows excellent promise for continuous environmental monitoring and air quality analysis.
Conversely, the recovery becomes slower in the order of PCN-222-Ni, PCN-222-Co, PCN-222-Fe, and PCN-222-Cu (Figure 3A−3D and Table S6). For example, after 1 min exposure of $0.15\mathrm{ppm}$ of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ the recovery times are 303, 315, 625, and $2628\ \mathrm{s}$ for PCN-222-Ni, PCN-222-Co, PCN-222-Fe, and PCN-222-Cu, respectively. The fastest recovery time is achieved for PCN-222-Ni, likely due to the lowest porosity (fewer gas molecules adsorbed within the pore) and weaker $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Zr}{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ interaction (vide infra) compared to those of the other three MOFs. In contrast, the longest recovery time of PCN-222-Cu should be attributed to the largest porosity and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zr}{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ interaction (vide infra) among the four MOFs. Note that being limited by our data-recording system of gassensing measurement, a short dynamic exposure time of 1 min has to be employed for PCN-222-M so as to achieve as many response/recovery cycles as possible for the reasonable assessment of the gas sensitivity.
Based on the above analysis of the four metalloporphyrinbased MOFs, PCN-222-Cu with the highest response to ultralow concentrations of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and PCN-222-Ni with the fastest recovery were selected to further study the reproducibility, stability, selectivity, and moisture resistance, which are the four key parameters for practical sensors. It can be seen qualitatively in Figure 4A,B that no significant changes were found in the sensitivity of the response and recovery characteristics for PCN-222-Cu $(0.004-0.04\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ) and PCN-222-Ni $\cdot0.02{-}0.2\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ ), indicating their acceptable reproducibility and stability. Significantly, the PCN222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni sensors exhibited exceptional longterm stability, as they nearly retained their original response values $\left(\left(I_{\mathrm{t}}-I_{0}\right)/I_{0}\times100\%\right)$ even after 210 days (Figure 4C). In addition, PXRD and FT-IR spectroscopy of PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni after $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensing measurement were also performed (Figure S5). It is not difficult to see that there is no change in the intensity and position of the peaks after the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensing measurement compared to that before the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensing measurement, which further demonstrated the excellent stability of PCN-222-M.
As displayed in Figures S6 and4D, both PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni showed no significant response to the other three hazardous gases $(\mathrm{NH}_{3},\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ and $S\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), implying their superselectivity. When considering the practical application of the sensor, the ambient humidity (RH) played a crucial role and significantly impacted the performance of the sensor.49,50 The PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni sensors exhibited outstanding reproducibilities for 0.01 and $0.04~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ respectively, under $60\%$ relative humidity (RH) (Figure 4E). Moreover, as shown in Figure 4F, the PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni sensors also showed a reproducible response to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ across a wide range of humidity levels $(0-100\%$ RH) with the standard deviation coefficients of 2.3 and $5.4\%$ , respectively, indicating their stable operation in various humidity environments.51,52
To further illustrate the reasons for the excellent moisture resistance of the devices, the contact angles of DI water droplets on the PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni films were investigated, as shown in the revised Supporting Information Figure S7. Large contact angles of $134^{\circ}$ and $125.5^{\circ}$ were found for the DI water droplet on the PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni films, respectively. The hydrophobic properties of PCN-222- Cu and PCN-222-Ni film effectively shield the adsorption of water molecules, making the constructed gas sensors highly resistant to moisture. From the above, the PCN-222-Cu- and PCN-222-Ni-based sensors possessed specific selectivity, high stability, and excellent moisture resistance, resulting in their great potential in the real-time monitoring of air quality.
Sensing Mechanism. As mentioned above, the four PCN222-M were typical organic−inorganic heterogeneous materials; therefore, it is very important to study the synergy of organic and inorganic units in the heterogeneous materials. As shown in Figure 5A, the energy levels of MTCPP $\mathrm{(M=Cu,Ni,}$ Fe, and Co) were provided via differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) (Figures S8 and S9).53−56 On the basis of the first reduction and oxidation potential revealed for the four MTCPP (Table S7), the LUMO energies are estimated to be ca. $-3.61$ (CuTCPP), $-4.28$ (NiTCPP), $-4.10$ (FeTCPP), and $-3.89$ (CoTCPP), respectively, suggesting their potential n-type semiconductor nature.57 The Fermi level of MTCPP was calculated from eq $1^{58-60}$ and Figure S9
$$
E_{\mathrm{f}}=-\big(h\nu-E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}\big)=-\big(21.2-E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}\big)
$$
The Fermi level increased in the order of NiTCPP $(-4.73)$ , FeTCPP $\left(-4.64\right)$ , CoTCPP (−4.49), and CuTCPP $\left(-4.08\right)$ .
Moreover, the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster usually exhibited p-type semiconductor characteristics, according to previous reports.57,61,62 Therefore, the Fermi levels of the four MTCPP were all much higher than that of the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, and the electrons could spontaneously be transferred from MPor to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster (while holes migrate into the MPor side) until the equilibrium state, where the Fermi levels are aligned.63 This definitely results in the same order of Fermi levels for the four PCN-222- M as those of the four MTCPP (i.e., PCN-222-Cu still possessed the highest value) (Figure 5B). Therefore, the electron transfer from PCN-222-Cu to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ was much easier, benefiting from its highest Fermi level, thus achieving the highest sensitivity and the lowest detection limit of PCN-222- Cu among the four MOFs. When subjected to strong oxidizing gases $(\mathrm{NO}_{2})$ , the PCN-222-M films are easily oxidized by $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ forming charge-transfer complexes, which inject additional holes in the films. The negative responses (where the currents decrease), as presented in Figure 3, revealed an exact $\mathfrak{n}$ -type behavior for the four PCN-222-M films, largely related to the respective precursor Mpor of n-type behavior. It is worth noting that, in any case, MPor, as a typical organic semiconductor, is more sensitive to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ than the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster. As a matter of fact, the sensing performance of PCN-222-Cu is significantly improved compared to those of metalloporphyrinbased molecular semiconductors.64−66 This is not only attributed to the unique one-dimensional mass transfer channel offered by PCN-222-M that can promote continuity of the conductive pathway of the sensing system, but more importantly the formation of $\mathbf{MPor/Zr}_{6}$ cluster hybrid materials that provide a free hole reservoir (buffer effect) at the $\mathbf{MPor/Zr}_{6}$ interface to boost the sensing performance.46
To further understand the relatively high sensing response of PCN-222-Cu, the interactions between $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and the MPor/ $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster of the four PCN-222-M compounds were evaluated by density functional theory (DFT). In addition, the electronic effect of PCN-222-M, perturbed by the binding of $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ was also investigated. The structure of the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ adsorption on PCN-222-M was optimized using the single-layer model of MOF in the $\mathrm{Dmol}^{3}$ module of Materials Studio 2019, as shown in Figure 6. The optimized structures of PCN-222-M $\mathbf{\left(M=\right}$ Cu, Ni, Fe, Co) are given in Figure $\mathrm{6A_{1}-D_{1}}$ . The Mulliken population analysis (Table S8) indicated that the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ absorbed on CuPor and the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster in PCN-222-Cu (Figure $6\mathrm{A}_{2},\mathrm{A}_{3},$ ) caused a decrease in positive charges on $\mathtt{C u}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}$ by 0.039 and 0.102, respectively. Accordingly, the total net charge of two $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Zr}}}$ active sites obtained from $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is 0.141. In contrast, for PCN-222-Ni, -Fe, and -Co, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ absorbed on the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster caused an increase in charges on $Z\mathbf{r}$ by 0.015, 0.004, and 0.056, respectively (i.e., $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ extracts electrons from the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster), although $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ can still donate electrons to $\mathrm{Ni,}$ Fe, and $C_{0}$ in NiPor, FePor, and CoPor units by 0.035, 0.028, and 0.083 (Figure $\mathrm{6B}_{1-3},6\mathrm{C}_{1-3},6\mathrm{D}_{1-3},$ and Table S8), respectively. As a result, the total net charges of $\mathrm{Ni}/$ Zr, Fe/Zr, and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zr}$ active sites obtained from $\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ are 0.02, 0.024, and 0.027 for PCN-222-Ni, -Fe, and -Co, respectively. Accordingly, among the four PCN-222-M, $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zr}$ of PCN$222\mathrm{-Cu}$ gains the most charge from $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ implying the strongest interaction between $\mathrm{{Cu}/\mathrm{{Zr}}}$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ while $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Zr}$ of PCN-222-Ni is the weakest one. This led to the highest response of PCN-222-Cu and the fastest recovery of PCN-222- Ni among the four PCN-222-M. Obviously, M of MPor dominated the electron transfer between the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . The synergistic effect of the CuPor and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster on PCN-222-Cu provided a superior sensing performance for $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . All of these findings indicated the significant role of $\mathrm{MPor/Zr}_{6}$ cluster interactions in modulating and amplifying the sensing response.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, four kinds of PCN-222-M $\mathbf{\hat{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Ni, Fe, Co)-based chemiresistive sensors were prepared through the QLS method for trace concentrations of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . Among them, PCN-222-Cu exhibited the highest sensitivity and the lowest LOD, while PCN-222-Ni showed the fastest recovery, which can be used for low-concentration detection and rapid detection of $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ respectively. Moreover, both PCN-222-Cu and PCN-222-Ni sensors possessed excellent selectivity, longterm stability, and moisture resistance, allowing the actual analysis for $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . The essential role of MPor units and the organic−inorganic hybrid material in modulating and enhancing the sensing response of PCN-222-M MOFs, formed through the coupling of MTCPP with the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, was supported by DFT calculations and energy level structures. This work not only achieved the best performance of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensing in terms of sensitivity and LOD but also provides guidance for the development of MOF-based chemiresistive sensors.
